# Standalone tablet setup app

Branch: `visionos-tablet-setup`, based on relay `465c11a`.

## Scope

Create a small native visionOS workflow test app, independent of PLANK desktop
streaming. Keep the reusable Swift setup/pairing layer here so the eventual
Vision Pro Client can consume the same implementation. Do not change the Host,
desktop Client, tablet capture worker or deployed relay service in this phase.

The operator accepts eight usable ExpressKeys as the initial pairing contract,
not a particular Wacom model. The existing daemon still selects a PTH-660 and
requires a separate `pair` process. Neither restriction is silently removed by
the test UI. Bluetooth here means tablet-to-Linux-relay, not relay-to-headset.

## First reviewable candidate

- Native SwiftUI wizard: choose relay, USB/Bluetooth setup, five ExpressKeys,
  completion and recovery. One clear task per page; no desktop-session controls.
- Default simulation with a persistent, prominent simulation banner. Exercise
  success, multiple devices, rejection, timeout, interrupted pairing and reconnect.
  Simulation never uses the network or writes live pairing credentials.
- Explicit live mode: manual endpoint; use the
  existing CPace confirmation protocol and OS Keychain. Never imply that a
  discovered advertisement proves identity. No protocol or crypto replacement.
- Live mode supports existing USB pairing only; automatic enrollment, Bluetooth
  management and daemon discovery advertisements remain future relay work.
- Reconnect check authenticates the saved relay through Noise without sending
  SESSION_READY or claiming a tablet. This is not a remote desktop or HID test.
- Separate app/Keychain namespace from the full Client. Forget removes only this
  app's local trust; explicitly explain that relay-side trust is not revoked.
- SDK/deployment target27, simulator/device builds and a macOS workflow preview.
  External signing configuration, pinned libsodium, no FFmpeg/Qt/SDL/Rust media.

## Validation

Pure state tests: legal transitions, invalid transitions, Bluetooth live gate,
mode isolation, cancellation, stale completions, failure retry and trust removal.
Use the actual existing C protocol/crypto tests, not a second implementation.
Compile the reusable layer and UI for the real visionOS SDK. Do not describe a
macOS preview or simulator compilation as headset qualification.

## Follow-up after workflow acceptance

1. Capability-based pad discovery: Wacom with eight usable ExpressKeys; select
   pad and pen from the same physical device. No model/product ID allowlist.
2. One daemon with automatic initial enrollment while unpaired and deliberate
   physical re-enrollment once paired. Bounded individual attempts and lockout.
3. Actual discovery advertisement and authenticated management/status contract.
4. BlueZ tablet setup: constrained provisional pairing, physical confirmation,
   persisted bond, Bluetooth-aware HID grouping and driver qualification.
5. USB/Bluetooth sleep/wake, unplug, replacement and multi-device hardware tests.
6. Import the reusable kit/UI into the maintained Vision Pro Client; no duplicate
   pairing state machine or fake Host/session handshake.

The headset installation step needs appropriate Apple development provisioning;
Developer ID macOS signing is not a visionOS installation method.
